Cells are the basic units of life. All living organisms are made of one or more cells. There are two main types of cells: prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells.
  Prokaryotic cells, such as bacteria, do not have a nucleus. Eukaryotic cells have a nucleus that contains the cell's genetic material. 
    Eukaryotic cells are found in organisms such as plants, animals, fungi, and protists.
Cells contain different organelles that perform specific jobs. The nucleus stores DNA and controls many of the cell's activities.
  Mitochondria produce energy for the cell through cellular respiration. Ribosomes make proteins, while the cell membrane controls what enters and leaves the cell. 
      Plant cells also contain chloroplasts, which allow plants to perform photosynthesis. 
  During photosynthesis, plants use sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide to produce glucose and oxygen.
